I keep the original tarball and extract it to the /tmp directory when 
needed. I wrote a script that first extracts the tarball to /tmp if the
original source directory doesn't exist there, and then extracts a diff
from my Nethack source work directory against it. This way I can be sure I
haven't accidentally modified anything in the original source tree.

> When I want to make a compiled version I copy that source tree and work on
> the copy. I wonder how one makes the patch-file unless they don't have
> original copy of the source tree?

When I wrote the early versions of my patches, I didn't have separate
directories for original and modified sources. Instead, I had copies of
the individual original source files that had been modified, in the same
directory as the modified version.
Then I just diffed each file individually against the original version,
appending the diffs to one file. Yes, that meant more manual work than
diffing whole directories and sometimes I may have forgotten some file
that should have been diffed..

